This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and prove the existence of a ground state solution when $\lambda\leq 0$ and $2<\mu<3$. Furthermore, for $\lambda>0$, we establish the existence of a normalized solution for the modified equation with an exponentially critical nonlinearity.

Recent analyses of lepton flavor universality (LFU) violating decays have suggested that new physics beyond the Standard Model might exist, potentially influencing multiple areas. To gain insights into these potential effects, we have explored their implications on the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ within the context of the $U_1$ leptoquark model. By linking the new physics interactions in $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ transitions to the known $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ couplings, we have determined how much the former could impact the latter. Utilizing the obtained new physics parameter space by performing a comprehensive fit to all relevant $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ data, we have derived predictions for several observables in the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$. We discovered that including only scalar operators in the analysis leads to incompatible results, whereas including vector operators allows for slightly suppressed values of certain semilocal hadronic decay constants and form factors. Additionally, we found that while the model can explain the observed values of several semilocal hadronic decay constants and form factors, it predicts a significantly lower value of $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ branching ratio in comparison to experimental measurements.

We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
  \min\left\{\int_{\Omega}f\left(x,y,\ DY\right)\,dx:\ Y\in K_\psi \right\},
  \end{equation*}
  where the functional $f$ grows sub-quadratically in its third argument and satisfies a convexity requirement, and the admissible set $K_\psi$ is defined by the obstacle condition $y\geq \psi$ almost everywhere in $\Omega$, with $y$ tracing close trajectories within the Sobolev space $W^{1,p}(\Omega)$, where $y$ has Dirichlet boundary conditions on $\Omega$ and $\psi$ has non-negative values and is a function of $W^{1,p}(\Omega)$. Our derivation comes from an alternative approach, based on a non-autonomous heat equation.
